! mcp_bootstrap_p124.strap -- MCP printed page 124, PDF p.131 of
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf.  THE HEADER WAS READ, NOT ASSUMED: the
! top line prints "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11011MCP   CARD ID
! EB000100   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER 124", and the
! right-hand column heading prints "LOCATION 216013".
!
! >>> THE PAGE IS FIFTY-SIX ROWS OF CODE AND NOTHING ELSE. <<<
! Every one of the 56 lines carries a LOCATION and BINARY OUTPUT.
! There are NO comment cards, NO SYN rows, NO DD/DRZ reservations and
! NO row whose location ends in "+".  The card-serial column runs
! EB000100 (line 1) to EB000150 (line 56) with no restart and no gap
! -- two statements share a card where the SIC/branch pair is one
! 64-bit instruction (lines 42/43 = EB000137) and where two index
! operations were punched together (lines 7/8 = EB000106, 9/10 =
! EB000107, 11/12 = EB000108, 28/29 = EB000124).  printed p.120's
! deck was still punching EA0001xx serials, so the EB deck opened
! somewhere in the three pages (121-123) that are not transcribed yet.
!
! WHAT THE PAGE DOES.  It is the channel/unit interrupt handler of
! the MCP supervisor: it sets the MCP and PP storage boundaries
! (lines 1-12), walks the channel and unit tables (13-26), decodes
! the four I/O indicator bits into four exit values (27-38), does a
! range check on the T.O.E. (39-41), returns through SIC/B,S DISP
! (42-43), and then handles the cancel / single-unit / malfunction /
! console-signal cases (44-56).
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THE ORIGIN IS ASSERTED, NOT CHECKED. <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THERE IS NO SLC ROW ON THIS PAGE.  The "SLC,(8)216013." written
! below is a HARD origin, and docs/transcription_workflow.md's
! "WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE" item 2 applies in full: a hard SLC
! OVERRIDES the arriving counter silently, so every !CHECK below
! would still pass if the origin were wrong.  Nothing in this deck
! tests it.  What DOES support it, by hand:
!
!   (a) THE PAGE'S OWN HEADER prints "LOCATION 216013" in the
!       right-hand column heading -- IBM's own assembler stating
!       where the page starts.  That is a second, independent
!       reading of the origin, printed on the page itself, and it
!       is the nearest thing to a printed "SLC,$" this page offers.
!   (b) BACKWARD JOIN.  docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_001-249.txt's block for
!       PDF p.130 (printed p.123) ends "5C# 216012.4C 215021.23 CD
!       V-I,9,S PROG S" -- a half word at 216012.40, so the counter
!       arrives at 216013.00, which is this page's line 1.
!   (c) FORWARD JOIN.  Line 56 is a full word at 216055.40, so the
!       counter leaves at 216056.40; the OCR block for PDF p.132
!       (printed p.125) opens "1# 216056.40 COCC10.26 50 LC,L,$L".
!
!   BOTH JOINS REST ON THE GARBLED FULL-VOLUME OCR, NOT ON A LANDED
!   DECK, because printed pp.121, 122 and 123 have not been
!   transcribed.  They are hand comparisons and no test consumes
!   them.  When p.123 and p.125 land, compare them against (b) and
!   (c) above.
!
! THE ADDRESS CHAIN CLOSES END TO END INSIDE THE PAGE.  First
! printed address 216013.00 (line 1), last 216055.40 (line 56);
! every row's printed address plus its own length equals the next
! row's printed address, with no gap and no overlap, across all
! fifty-six.  Twenty rows are full words (two printed half-words)
! and thirty-six are half words.
!
! THREE ROWS CARRY A "*" IN THE ONE-CHARACTER FLAG COLUMN AFTER THE
! LOCATION: lines 3, 22 and 38.  Recorded, not explained -- p.103
! and p.120 both carry listing marks nobody has accounted for.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS -- WHERE EACH SPELLING AND ADDRESS CAME FROM
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FOURTEEN OF THE TWENTY-THREE ARE INHERITED FROM LANDED DECKS, and
! that is why the deck does not have to back-derive them:
!
!   SBAMCP  215003.00   p.103 !SYM (line 31 of that page)
!   SBAPP   215005.00   p.103 !SYM
!   SCHANS  215002.00   p.103 !SYM (SYN,SXCHAN there)
!   SBNDRS  215224.00   p.116 !SYM; p.117 writes SYN,(8)215224.0
!   SLRBU   215324.00   p.116 !SYM; p.117 writes SYN,(8)215324.0
!   SLRMCP  215362.00   p.116 !SYM; p.117 writes SYN,(8)215362.0
!   SLRPP   215266.00   p.116 !SYM
!   STLR    215230.00   p.116 !SYM; p.117/p.118 write SYN,(8)215230.0
!   SCHANN  SYN(BU,7),0.12    p.101 line 5  -CHANNEL NUMBER
!   SCCW    SYN(BU,64),4.0    p.102 line 15 -COPIED CONTROL WORD
!   SIOIND  SYN(BU,5),1.09    p.102 line 7  -IO INDICATORS
!   SMULTI  SYN(BU,1),0.27    p.99  line 24 -MULTI-UNIT CHAN
!   SUNSUP  SYN(BU,1),0.30    p.99  line 28 -UNIT SUPPRESSION
!   SUNASG  SYN(BU,1),0.29    p.99  line 27 -UNIT ASSIGNMENT
!   SUNITK  SYN(BU,4),0.59    p.99  line 40 -UNIT COUNT
!   SPPTNG  SYN,(16)1005.0    p.107 line 10 - 5 ILLEGAL PP T.O.E.
!       (line 41's LVI prints the WORD 010005; see the note below --
!        it does NOT corroborate the bit offset)
!   SDISIC  (8)217451.40      p.103/p.119/p.120
!   SDISP   (8)217372.00      p.119/p.120
!
! tests/check_symbol_spellings.py was run with this deck added to the
! deck set: 1047 symbol definitions, no symbol spelled two ways.
!
! THE FIVE THIS PAGE INTRODUCES, and how far each is pinned:
!
!   KSUPP   216242.00  Corroborated OFF THE PAGE.  The full-volume
!       OCR's block for PDF p.135 (printed p.128) prints "2% 216242.CC
!       216246.7C CO  K SUPP  NOP,K COMM R" -- the defining card, at
!       216242.00, spelled "K SUPP".  This page prints it closed up,
!       "KSUPP", on lines 27 and 47; blanks are not part of a STRAP
!       name, so the two are the same symbol.  Lines 27 and 47 are
!       both branch-family rows, whose printed offset DOES carry bit
!       32 (that is how PCONE1 was caught at .40 below), and both
!       print 216242.34 with no 0o40 -- so the LOW half word is a
!       reading here, not a convention; bits 0-31 are not.  The
!       OCR'd defining row agrees.
!   KKK     215730.00  Corroborated: the OCR's block for PDF p.129
!       (printed p.122)
!       prints "2S% 215730.CCG ... KKK --L(BU,3)S UNIT($1C),-18" at
!       215730.00.  Line 52's "B,K KK" prints 215730.10, i.e. no
!       half-word bit, so .00 is a reading and not a convention here.
!   KDOCL   215732.00  Defining card on printed p.122 (PDF p.129),
!       "K DOCL  LV,12,S UN A($11)   -OBTAIN UNIT AREA TABLE".  See
!       the GLYPHS note below -- the fourth character is a C and not
!       an O, read at 1800 dpi against the closed O beside it.
!   PCONE1  216620.40  >>> ITS HALF WORD IS OBSERVABLE AND IT IS THE
!       UPPER ONE. <<<  Line 51 prints "216620.72 C2"; the same
!       instruction with the symbol at 216620.00 assembles .32, so
!       the printed 0o40 is the half-word select.  Confirmed
!       independently: the OCR's block for PDF p.142 (printed p.135)
!       prints an SLC row at 216620.40 and then "216620.4C ...
!       P CON 1  CM0000(BU,3),
!       S UNIT($10)", and the comment cards above it read "P CON E1
!       IS THE ENTRY PT FROM RECEPTOR IF CS INTERRUPT ON..." and
!       "P CON E2 IS THE ENTRY PT IF...".  THE COMMENT COLUMN IS THE
!       ORACLE FOR THE NAME: it is P CON E1, with an E, even though
!       the NAME column OCRs as "P CON 1".
!   KSINGL 216050.00 and KSIGNL 216054.00 are DEFINED ON THIS PAGE,
!       and both are corroborated by references from printed p.122
!       (OCR block for PDF p.129): the OCR there prints "...216050.34 OC BZB,S MULTI($10),
!       K SINGL" and "...216054.34 C2 BRZ,K SIGNL".  Two
!       near-identical names on one page, each confirmed by a
!       different caller at the right address.
!
! >>> WHERE A BIT OFFSET IS AND IS NOT OBSERVABLE HERE. <<<  For the
! plain branches (B, lines 4/45/49/52/43) and the branch-on-bit rows,
! the printed offset carries bit 32 of the target, so .00 vs .40 IS
! read, not assumed -- that is how PCONE1 was caught at .40.  What is
! NOT observable is bits 0-31: every value from .00 to .37 encodes
! identically, so KSUPP, KKK, KDOCL, SDISP and the rest are pinned to
! the HALF WORD and their ".00" is a convention.  The index-arithmetic
! rows (LV/V+/KV/LC/...) print a WORD address and a field that is the
! J register doubled, so they pin nothing below the word.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPHS -- FIVE READINGS THAT CHANGED ON THE SECOND LOOK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! 1. RNGTOE, NOT RNGTCE.  Lines 8, 12 and 39 print this name.  Line
!    12 and line 39 print the fifth character OPEN, which is what
!    tesseract and a 600-dpi eye both call a C.  LINE 8 PRINTS IT
!    CLOSED at 1800 dpi ("BXL,RNGTOE").  Ink dropout can open a
!    closed glyph but never closes an open one, so one closed
!    instance settles all three: the letter is O.  The name then
!    reads as "RaNGe T.O.E.", and printed p.107 line 10's comment
!    for SPPTNG is "- 5 ILLEGAL PP T.O.E.", which is the same table.
! 2. THE V+ ROWS PRINT B0, NOT 80.  Lines 2, 5, 16 and 17 were first
!    read "80" at 600 dpi.  At 1800 dpi all four show the straight
!    left stem of a B, and line 20's "BC" (V+ with an index of 12)
!    is the same first nibble beside an unambiguous C.  The
!    assembler agrees.  B and 8 differ by one stroke and this page
!    is why mcp_crop.sh renders the code column at 900 dpi.
! 3. LINES 27, 51 AND 55 END IN C0/C2, NOT 00/02.  These were read
!    correctly at 900 dpi and then WRONGLY "corrected" to 00 by
!    reasoning from the neighbouring BZB rows.  At 1800 dpi the
!    first byte glyph has a wide right-hand opening where line 52's
!    "00" and line 53's "02" in the same column are closed.  The
!    lesson is the one docs/transcription_workflow.md already
!    states: settle the byte on what produced it, and an argument
!    from a DIFFERENT instruction is not that.
! 4. K DOCL vs K DODO.  Both names contain a round glyph with a gap.
!    They are different letters, and the discriminator is the SIZE
!    of the gap, read at 1800 dpi against the closed O in the same
!    name: K DODO's fourth character has a hairline break and
!    matches its own second character (a closed O); K DOCL's fourth
!    character has an opening spanning a third of the circle and is
!    plainly wider than the closed O two places before it.  Both
!    names rest on ink alone -- nothing on this page or any landed
!    deck references either -- so this is the weakest reading in the
!    deck and is flagged as such.  K DOCL's defining card on printed
!    p.122 prints the same shapes, which is a second printing but
!    NOT a second drum, so it is corroboration of a weak kind.
!    >>> K DODO IS THE ONE SYMBOL IN THIS DECK THAT NOTHING
!    CORROBORATES AT ALL. <<<  K DOCL and K KK have defining cards on
!    printed p.122, K SUPP has one on printed p.128, K SINGL and
!    K SIGNL have callers on printed p.122, and every S-prefixed
!    external is a landed deck's spelling.  K DODO is defined here,
!    referenced only here (line 18), and rests on ink alone.
! 5. "C ERROR" REALLY DOES BEGIN WITH C, on a page where every other
!    label begins with K or R.  The glyph's opening is as wide as
!    the C in "K C SIGL" on the line below and much wider than the
!    closed O's in the word ERROR beside it.  It is printed the same
!    way in lines 31 and 32's operands.
! Also settled by zoom: line 15's index is ($13), not the ($12) the
! full-volume OCR reads; the assembled word agrees with ($13).
! Lines 31-34 are ONE symbol, S IO IND, indexed +.0/+.1/+.2/+.3.
! Line 34 prints it "S IC IND" to the eye; the other three print the
! O, and p.102's deck already defines S IO IND at 000001.11.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ASSEMBLER LIMITATIONS -- THREE, ALL REPORTED AND NOT FIXED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! NOTHING UNDER strap/ WAS MODIFIED.  Each printed form below WAS
! TESTED first and really is rejected; each substitute assembles
! BIT-IDENTICALLY (its row's !CHECK passes against the printed
! binary) and the printed form is kept in the row's note.
!
! (1) AN ADDRESS FIELD THAT IS NOTHING BUT A PARENTHETICAL INDEX.
!     Line 13 prints "LV,$12,($2)".  strap_asm raises
!     "empty expression" -- eval_addr_field is handed "" and
!     tokenize_expr refuses it.  The deck writes "LV, $12, ($2)",
!     which prints the same 000000.30 32.  This is NOT the
!     "(CC*)DD" parenthetical-entry gap and not the ".55(.63)21"
!     case the brief mentions (that one assembles fine); it is a
!     one-line gap in the address-field parser, where an omitted
!     base should default to 0.
!
! (2) A CONNECTIVE WITH THE IMMEDIATE "I" INFIXED, NOT SUFFIXED.
!     Line 26 prints "CTI0001(BU,5),(2)11101".  classify_connective
!     accepts C/CM/CT plus exactly four binary digits, and the
!     immediate-suffix retry at strap_asm.py:1459 only strips a
!     TRAILING "I", so "CTI0001" matches neither and raises a bare
!     KeyError 'CTI0001'.  "CT0001I" -- the same operation with the
!     I at the end -- assembles and prints the identical
!     720000.00 80 405000.03 70, so the encoder is complete and only
!     the mnemonic grammar is short.  C22-6531 Appendix A footnote 3
!     ("may be suffixed by the letter I") is already cited in that
!     function; MCP's own listing shows IBM's assembler ALSO took it
!     infixed, between the connective's letters and its four bits.
!
! (3) TRANSMIT'S J FIELD WILL NOT TAKE A REGISTER NAME.
!     Line 40 prints "T,$12,SLRPP,STLR".  strap_asm evaluates T's J
!     with eval_expr, which turns "$12" into index register 12's BIT
!     ADDRESS (1792) and then refuses it: "T's J field is 4 bits
!     (pocket card, bits 51-54); 1792 does not fit".  Written "T, 12,
!     SLRPP, STLR" it assembles to the printed 215266.00 80
!     215230.30 20, so the J really is the register NUMBER and the
!     fix is one call -- eval_reg, as the count-branch and
!     branch-on-indicator paths a few lines above already use.
!     A REFUSAL, NOT A WRONG ANSWER, which is the safe failure.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THIS DECK DOES NOT ESTABLISH
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! * The origin (see above) -- a hard SLC cannot disagree with the
!   counter it replaces.
! * Bits 0-31 of every external SYN'd off a branch.
! * The spellings K DODO and K DOCL, which nothing references.
! * $MASK, $IND and $15 are strap_asm's own register names; the page
!   prints them that way and the rows assemble bit-exactly, but no
!   primary source was consulted for them in this transcription.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------

